; A BUILD RETURNS A PRODUCT: the table, and how much of it was written.
; expect: 6307 8408 10809
; inputs: 0 2 5
;
; tables.md §2.5, ADR 0031. "Build to a bound and return the count" was named in
; tables.md §14.3 as the answer to accumulation whose size is not known first,
; and could not be written: a `build` had one way out, so the count had none.
; Here the scope's loop yields `(tuple b k)` from two exits, and the pattern
; takes it apart AFTER the scope: `b` is the frozen table, and `k` how many of
; its cells were stored.
;
; The analyses must carry both across the tuple (the component law): `len b` is
; the scope's size, so `(b j)` is in bounds; `k` is at most `cap`, joined over
; the two exits; and b's cells hold values below 20 + n. Every operation is
; proven, and nothing is declared but the input's range.
;
; The multiples of 3 below 20 + n, summed, times 100, plus how many:
;   n=0: 0 3 … 18, seven of them, sum 63   -> 6307
;   n=2: 0 3 … 21, eight, sum 84           -> 8408
;   n=5: 0 3 … 24, nine, sum 108           -> 10809

(def cap 32)

(sig run ((n (int 0 100))) int)
(def run (n)
  (let (tuple b k)
       (build b cap
         (loop ((b b) (i 0) (k 0))
           (>= i (+ 20 n))  (tuple b k)
           (>= k cap)       (tuple b k)
           (= (% i 3) 0)    (again (set b k i) (+ i 1) (+ k 1))
           else             (again b (+ i 1) k)))
    (loop ((s 0) (j 0))
      (>= j k)        (+ (* s 100) k)
      (>= j (len b))  (+ (* s 100) k)
      else            (again (+ s (b j)) (+ j 1)))))
